## SessionFactory vs Session

```java
// Hibernate native API (rarely used directly in Spring apps)
SessionFactory sessionFactory = configuration.buildSessionFactory();
// One per application — expensive to build
// Thread-safe — store in a singleton

try (Session session = sessionFactory.openSession()) {
    // One per unit of work — cheap to create
    // NOT thread-safe — don't share across threads
    Transaction tx = session.beginTransaction();
    Order order = session.get(Order.class, 1L);
    tx.commit();
}
// Session closes, JDBC connection returned to pool
```

## JPA Equivalents (Spring Standard)

```java
// EntityManagerFactory = JPA spec wrapper over SessionFactory
EntityManagerFactory emf;  // Spring creates this from DataSource + JPA config

// EntityManager = JPA spec wrapper over Session
EntityManager em;  // Spring creates per transaction via @Transactional

// You can unwrap to get Hibernate native objects if needed:
Session session = em.unwrap(Session.class);
SessionFactory sf = emf.unwrap(SessionFactory.class);
```

## Spring's @Transactional and Session Lifecycle

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {

    @Autowired
    private EntityManager em;  // Thread-bound proxy — not the actual instance
    // Each method call on em delegates to the current thread's EntityManager

    @Transactional
    public Order createOrder(OrderRequest req) {
        // Spring: open EntityManager (Session), begin transaction
        Order order = new Order(req);
        em.persist(order);          // Added to first-level cache
        return order;
        // Spring: commit transaction, close EntityManager (Session)
        // JDBC connection returned to pool
    }

    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    public Order findById(Long id) {
        // Spring: open EntityManager, mark as read-only
        return em.find(Order.class, id);  // Checks L1 cache first, then DB
        // Spring: commit (no flush), close EntityManager
    }
}
```

## Session Scope and Thread Safety

```java
// WRONG: Storing Session in a field
@Component
public class BadService {
    @Autowired
    private EntityManager em;  // This is safe — it's a proxy

    private Session session;  // DANGEROUS — holds an actual Session

    @Transactional
    public void doWork() {
        session = em.unwrap(Session.class);  // Now bound to this transaction
        // Storing session as a field is dangerous — next request reuses session
        // from a closed/committed transaction
    }
}

// CORRECT: Use EntityManager proxy — Spring handles lifecycle
@Component
public class GoodService {
    @Autowired
    private EntityManager em;  // Proxy — always delegates to current tx's EM

    @Transactional
    public void doWork() {
        em.persist(new Order());  // Safely routes to current transaction's EM
    }
}
```

## Production Reality

In a Spring application, you never need to manage `SessionFactory`/`Session` directly. Let Spring's `@Transactional` handle the lifecycle. The key understanding for interviews is knowing why `EntityManager` needs to be a proxy (thread-safety) and why you shouldn't hold references to the underlying Session across transaction boundaries.

## Persistence Context Lifecycle

```
Transaction begins → Persistence Context created (empty)
  em.find(Order.class, 1L)
    → Query DB: SELECT * FROM orders WHERE id = 1
    → Load Order object
    → Store snapshot {status: PENDING, amount: 100.0, ...}
    → Store entity reference
    → Return Order ("managed" state)
  order.setStatus(SHIPPED)     ← You change the field
  // No SQL yet!
  Flush triggered:
    → Compare: {status: SHIPPED, amount: 100.0, ...}
             vs snapshot: {status: PENDING, amount: 100.0, ...}
    → Difference found: status changed
    → Generate: UPDATE orders SET status = 'SHIPPED' WHERE id = 1
Transaction commits → Persistence Context cleared
```

## Dirty Checking in Code

```java
@Transactional
public void promoteOrder(Long id) {
    Order order = orderRepo.findById(id).orElseThrow();
    // order is MANAGED — tracked by persistence context

    order.setStatus(OrderStatus.SHIPPED);  // Mark as dirty
    order.setUpdatedAt(LocalDateTime.now());

    // NO need to call orderRepo.save(order) !
    // Hibernate detects changes at flush time
    // On commit: UPDATE orders SET status='SHIPPED', updated_at=? WHERE id=?
}

// Contrast with DETACHED entity:
@Transactional
public void updateDetached(Order detachedOrder) {
    // detachedOrder came from outside — not managed!
    // Option 1: merge (attaches and uses dirty checking)
    Order managed = em.merge(detachedOrder);
    // Option 2: use save() in Spring Data
    orderRepo.save(detachedOrder);  // If id exists → merge; else → persist
}
```

## Flush Modes

```java
// AUTO (default): flush before queries to avoid stale reads
@Transactional
public void workWithAutoFlush() {
    order.setStatus(SHIPPED);          // entity dirtied
    // Before this query, Hibernate flushes (executes UPDATE)
    List<Order> shipped = orderRepo.findByStatus(SHIPPED);  // Includes our change
}

// COMMIT: flush only on commit (more performant, can cause stale reads)
em.setFlushMode(FlushModeType.COMMIT);

// MANUAL: explicit flush control
em.setFlushMode(FlushModeType.MANUAL);
em.flush();  // Only updates when you explicitly call this
```

## ReadOnly Optimization

```java
// Without readOnly:
@Transactional
public List<Order> findAll() {
    // Hibernate: 1) loads all orders, 2) takes snapshots, 3) flushes (dirty check) at commit
    // Dirty check scans ALL loaded entities — expensive with large result sets
}

// With readOnly:
@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public List<Order> findAll() {
    // Hibernate: 1) loads all orders, skips snapshot taking
    // No dirty check at commit — faster, less memory
    // DB: may set read-only on JDBC connection (no DML allowed)
}
```

## Entity States

```
Transient: new Order() — not in PC, not in DB
Managed: em.persist(o) or em.find() — tracked by PC
Detached: outside transaction — was managed, now not tracked
Removed: em.remove(o) — will be deleted on flush
```

## Production Reality

Always annotate read-only service methods with `@Transactional(readOnly = true)`. For large collection loads, the dirty checking overhead can be significant. Understand that modifying a managed entity without calling `save()` is intentional — but it can cause unexpected UPDATEs if you're not aware of transaction boundaries.

## How Proxies Work

```java
Order order = em.find(Order.class, 1L);
// order.user is a proxy (FetchType.LAZY)
User userProxy = order.getUser();

System.out.println(userProxy.getClass());
// Output: class com.example.User$HibernateProxyXXXX
//         (ByteBuddy-generated subclass, not User.class)

System.out.println(userProxy.getId());
// Output: 5  (ID is stored in proxy — no DB query)

System.out.println(userProxy.getName());
// Now: SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = 5 (triggers query)
// Output: "John Doe"
```

## getClass() Problem in equals()

```java
// WRONG: breaks with proxies
@Override
public boolean equals(Object obj) {
    if (this == obj) return true;
    if (obj == null || getClass() != obj.getClass()) return false;  // ← Problem!
    User other = (User) obj;
    return Objects.equals(id, other.id);
}
// order.getUser().equals(loadedUser) → false!
// proxy.getClass() != User.class

// CORRECT: use instanceof
@Override
public boolean equals(Object obj) {
    if (this == obj) return true;
    if (!(obj instanceof User)) return false;  // proxy IS an instanceof User
    User other = (User) obj;
    return Objects.equals(id, other.id);
}
```

## Checking Proxy State

```java
Order order = orderRepo.findById(1L).orElseThrow();
User userProxy = order.getUser();

// Check if initialized (loaded from DB)
boolean loaded = Hibernate.isInitialized(userProxy);  // false — not yet queried

// Force initialize
Hibernate.initialize(userProxy);
loaded = Hibernate.isInitialized(userProxy);  // true now

// Unwrap proxy to get actual entity
User actualUser = Hibernate.unproxy(userProxy, User.class);
System.out.println(actualUser.getClass());  // User.class
```

## em.find() vs em.getReference()

```java
// em.find(): immediately queries DB, returns null if not found
User user = em.find(User.class, 99L);  // SELECT ... WHERE id = 99
// user == null if not found

// em.getReference(): returns proxy immediately — no DB query
User userRef = em.getReference(User.class, 99L);
// userRef is a proxy — no DB query yet
// Accessing any non-id field: SELECT...
// If id 99 doesn't exist: EntityNotFoundException on first access

// Use getReference() for setting FK relationships:
order.setUser(em.getReference(User.class, userId));
// No SELECT needed — just sets the FK, inserts with the ID
```

## Collection Proxies

```java
// Collections also use proxies — PersistentBag, PersistentSet, PersistentList
Order order = orderRepo.findById(1L).orElseThrow();
List<Item> items = order.getItems();
System.out.println(items.getClass());
// org.hibernate.collection.spi.PersistentBag

// Not queried yet:
Hibernate.isInitialized(items);  // false

// Triggers SELECT:
int size = items.size();  // Collection loaded here
Hibernate.isInitialized(items);  // true
```

## Production Reality

Always implement `equals()` and `hashCode()` using `instanceof` in entities, not `getClass()`. Never use entity class identity for equality — use business identity (typically the ID). Also: never call `getClass()` on an entity in a Hibernate context when you want to check the actual business type — use `instanceof` or `Hibernate.unproxy()`.

## Flush vs Commit

```
Transaction timeline:
  BEGIN TX
    em.persist(newOrder)       → queued: INSERT
    order.setStatus(SHIPPED)   → queued: UPDATE
    ---------- FLUSH -----------
    → execute INSERT INTO orders...
    → execute UPDATE orders SET status...
    → SQL sent to DB, but NOT committed

    findByStatus(SHIPPED)  ← would read the unflushed update → flush first!
  ---------- COMMIT -----------
    → DB makes changes permanent
  ---------- OR ROLLBACK --------
    → DB discards all changes (even flushed ones!)
```

## FlushMode Comparison

```java
// AUTO (default): flush before queries
@Transactional
public void processOrders() {
    Order order = orderRepo.findById(1L).orElseThrow();
    order.setStatus(SHIPPED);  // dirty

    // Hibernate checks: will this query read from 'orders'? Yes.
    // → Flush first: UPDATE orders SET status='SHIPPED' WHERE id=1
    List<Order> shipped = orderRepo.findByStatus(SHIPPED);  // Sees the change
    // shipped contains our modified order
}

// COMMIT: flush only at commit
@Transactional
public void processOrdersCommitMode() {
    EntityManagerHolder holder = (EntityManagerHolder)
        TransactionSynchronizationManager.getResource(emf);
    holder.getEntityManager().setFlushMode(FlushModeType.COMMIT);

    Order order = orderRepo.findById(1L).orElseThrow();
    order.setStatus(SHIPPED);

    // No flush before query!
    List<Order> shipped = orderRepo.findByStatus(SHIPPED);
    // shipped may NOT contain our modified order (stale read within TX)
    // But queries run faster — no dirty check
}
```

## @Modifying and Stale Persistence Context

```java
// Bulk updates bypass entity tracking
@Transactional
public void bulkUpdate() {
    Order order = orderRepo.findById(1L).orElseThrow();
    System.out.println(order.getStatus());  // PENDING

    // Direct UPDATE bypasses persistence context:
    orderRepo.updateStatusBulk(OrderStatus.SHIPPED);
    // @Query("UPDATE Order o SET o.status = :status")
    // @Modifying — executes SQL directly

    System.out.println(order.getStatus());  // Still PENDING! (stale in PC)
    // PC not aware of bulk update
}

// Fix: clear PC after @Modifying
@Modifying(clearAutomatically = true)  // Clears PC after execution
@Query("UPDATE Order o SET o.status = :status")
void updateStatusBulk(@Param("status") OrderStatus status);
```

## Explicit Flush

```java
@Transactional
public void manualControl() {
    em.setFlushMode(FlushModeType.MANUAL);

    // Many operations — no auto flush
    for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
        em.persist(new Order());
        if (i % 50 == 0) {
            em.flush();   // Explicit flush every 50
            em.clear();   // Clear PC to free memory
        }
    }
    // Final flush on commit
}
// Pattern: batch processing with manual flush+clear
```

## Production Reality

For batch processing large datasets, use `FlushMode.MANUAL` with explicit `flush()` and `clear()` every N records to prevent out-of-memory errors and reduce flush overhead. For normal transactional code, AUTO is correct. Understanding the difference between flush and commit is essential for explaining transaction rollback behavior.

## Level 1 Cache (Session/Persistence Context)

```java
@Transactional
public void demonstrateL1Cache() {
    // Query 1: SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = 1
    User user1 = em.find(User.class, 1L);

    // NO query! L1 cache hit — same persistence context:
    User user2 = em.find(User.class, 1L);

    System.out.println(user1 == user2);  // true — same object reference

    // L1 cache cleared on transaction end
}

// New transaction: L1 is empty again
@Transactional
public void nextTransaction() {
    // Query 2: SELECT again — previous L1 cache is gone
    User user = em.find(User.class, 1L);
}
```

## Level 2 Cache (SessionFactory/Application Scope)

```xml
<!-- pom.xml -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.hibernate.orm</groupId>
    <artifactId>hibernate-jcache</artifactId>
</dependency>
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.ehcache</groupId>
    <artifactId>ehcache</artifactId>
</dependency>
```

```properties
spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.cache.use_second_level_cache=true
spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.cache.region.factory_class=\
  org.hibernate.cache.jcache.JCacheRegionFactory
```

```java
@Entity
@Cache(usage = CacheConcurrencyStrategy.READ_WRITE)
public class Category {
    @Id @GeneratedValue
    private Long id;
    private String name;
}

// Transaction 1:
Category cat = em.find(Category.class, 1L);  // DB query, populates L2
// Transaction 1 ends — L1 gone, but L2 still has it

// Transaction 2 (new session):
Category cat2 = em.find(Category.class, 1L);  // L2 HIT — no DB query!
```

## Query Cache

```java
// What query cache stores:
// Key: JPQL string + parameter values
// Value: list of entity IDs
// Entities themselves stored in L2 cache

@Query("SELECT c FROM Category c WHERE c.active = true ORDER BY c.sortOrder")
@QueryHints(@QueryHint(name = "org.hibernate.cacheable", value = "true"))
List<Category> findAllActive();

// First call: DB query, caches {active=true} → [id1, id2, id3, ...]
// Second call: cache hit → lookup id1, id2, id3 from L2 (or DB if not in L2)
// If ANY Category is inserted/updated: query cache for Category queries is invalidated!
```

## Cache Strategy Choices

```
Good L2 candidates:
  - Country list, currency list, category tree
  - Configuration/settings entities
  - Rarely updated reference data
  - Entities read far more than written

Bad L2 candidates:
  - User balances, order status (high-write)
  - Large entities with many associations
  - Multi-datacenter setups without distributed cache
  - Entities where stale reads are unacceptable
```

## Monitoring Cache Effectiveness

```properties
spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.generate_statistics=true
```

```
SecondLevelCacheStatistics:
  putCount = 150
  hitCount = 4500
  missCount = 150
  hitRatio = 4500 / (4500 + 150) = 96.7%  ← Good cache
```

## Production Reality

In a single-instance app, L2 cache is straightforward. In a cluster, you need a distributed cache provider (Redis, Hazelcast) or accept that each instance has its own L2 cache and serves slightly stale data from different instances. For most microservices, a well-indexed database with connection pooling is sufficient — introduce L2 cache only when profiling shows a specific entity is causing DB bottlenecks at high read rates.

## Optimistic Locking

```java
@Entity
public class Order {
    @Id @GeneratedValue
    private Long id;

    private OrderStatus status;

    @Version  // Hibernate manages this field
    private int version;
    // Or: @Version private Instant lastModified;
}

// Transaction 1: read order (version = 3)
Order order = orderRepo.findById(1L).orElseThrow();
// order.version == 3

// Transaction 2 (concurrent): also reads (version = 3)
// Transaction 2 modifies and commits: version becomes 4 in DB

// Transaction 1: modifies and tries to commit
// Hibernate executes:
// UPDATE orders SET status='SHIPPED', version=4 WHERE id=1 AND version=3
// Affected rows: 0 (version in DB is now 4)
// → Throws OptimisticLockException!

// Retry logic:
@Retryable(value = ObjectOptimisticLockingFailureException.class, maxAttempts = 3)
public void updateOrder(Long id, OrderStatus status) {
    Order order = orderRepo.findById(id).orElseThrow();
    order.setStatus(status);
    // @Transactional auto-commits, may retry on conflict
}
```

## Pessimistic Locking

```java
// LockModeType.PESSIMISTIC_WRITE → SELECT ... FOR UPDATE
Optional<Order> findById(Long id);

// In repository or service:
@Transactional
public void reserveInventory(Long productId, int quantity) {
    // Acquires DB lock — other transactions must wait
    Product product = em.find(Product.class, productId, LockModeType.PESSIMISTIC_WRITE);
    // or:
    Product product2 = productRepo.findById(productId, LockModeType.PESSIMISTIC_WRITE);

    if (product.getStock() < quantity) {
        throw new InsufficientStockException();
    }
    product.setStock(product.getStock() - quantity);
    // Lock released on commit — other transactions can proceed
}

// In Spring Data:
@Lock(LockModeType.PESSIMISTIC_WRITE)
@Query("SELECT p FROM Product p WHERE p.id = :id")
Optional<Product> findByIdForUpdate(@Param("id") Long id);
```

## Timeout Configuration

```java
// Pessimistic lock with timeout (avoid indefinite wait)
@Lock(LockModeType.PESSIMISTIC_WRITE)
@QueryHints(@QueryHint(name = "javax.persistence.lock.timeout", value = "5000"))
Optional<Product> findByIdForUpdate(@Param("id") Long id);
// Throws LockTimeoutException if lock not acquired within 5 seconds
```

## Decision Guide

```
Use Optimistic Locking when:
  - Read-modify-write with low conflict probability
  - Web forms: user reads data, edits, submits 30 seconds later
  - Most CRUD operations
  - Scalability is critical

Use Pessimistic Locking when:
  - Inventory reservation: 100 users buying last item simultaneously
  - Financial transactions with strict consistency
  - Short, fast operations where lock contention is brief
  - Conflict on optimistic lock means operation must fail (not retry)
```

## Production Reality

Start with optimistic locking and `@Version` for everything. Add pessimistic locking only for specific high-contention operations proven by testing (like flash sale inventory). Pessimistic locks held for too long cause connection pool exhaustion in high-traffic scenarios.

## JPA Lifecycle Callbacks (Simpler — Prefer These)

```java
@Entity
@EntityListeners(AuditListener.class)  // Delegate to separate class
public class Order {
    @CreatedDate
    private LocalDateTime createdAt;

    @LastModifiedDate
    private LocalDateTime updatedAt;

    @CreatedBy
    private String createdBy;

    @PrePersist
    protected void onPrePersist() {
        // Called before INSERT — set defaults
        if (this.status == null) this.status = OrderStatus.PENDING;
    }

    @PreUpdate
    protected void onPreUpdate() {
        // Called before UPDATE
        this.updatedAt = LocalDateTime.now();
    }

    @PostLoad
    protected void onPostLoad() {
        // Called after SELECT — decrypt, compute fields
    }

    @PreRemove
    protected void onPreRemove() {
        // Called before DELETE — validation
        if (this.status == OrderStatus.SHIPPED) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Cannot delete shipped order");
        }
    }
}
```

## Spring Data Auditing (Simplest Approach)

```java
@EnableJpaAuditing
@Configuration
public class JpaConfig {
    @Bean
    public AuditorAware<String> auditorProvider() {
        return () -> Optional.ofNullable(SecurityContextHolder.getContext())
            .map(ctx -> ctx.getAuthentication())
            .filter(auth -> auth != null && auth.isAuthenticated())
            .map(Authentication::getName);
    }
}

@Entity
@EntityListeners(AuditingEntityListener.class)
public class Order {
    @CreatedDate private LocalDateTime createdAt;
    @LastModifiedDate private LocalDateTime updatedAt;
    @CreatedBy private String createdBy;
    @LastModifiedBy private String updatedBy;
    // All managed automatically — no code needed
}
```

## Hibernate EmptyInterceptor (For Cross-Entity Concerns)

```java
@Component
public class AuditInterceptor extends EmptyInterceptor {

    @Override
    public boolean onSave(Object entity, Serializable id, Object[] state,
                          String[] propertyNames, Type[] types) {
        // Called before INSERT for any entity
        System.out.println("Inserting: " + entity.getClass().getSimpleName() + " id=" + id);
        return false;  // false = no state change
    }

    @Override
    public boolean onFlushDirty(Object entity, Serializable id,
                                Object[] currentState, Object[] previousState,
                                String[] propertyNames, Type[] types) {
        // Called for every dirty entity at flush — for all types
        auditLog(entity, previousState, currentState, propertyNames);
        return false;
    }
}

// Register with SessionFactory:
@Bean
public HibernatePropertiesCustomizer hibernateInterceptorCustomizer(
        AuditInterceptor interceptor) {
    return properties -> properties.put(
        "hibernate.session_factory.interceptor", interceptor
    );
}
```

## Soft Delete Filter (Hibernate Filter)

```java
@Entity
@FilterDef(name = "softDeleteFilter", parameters = @ParamDef(name = "deleted", type = Boolean.class))
@Filter(name = "softDeleteFilter", condition = "deleted = :deleted")
public class Order {
    private boolean deleted = false;
    // Other fields
}

// Enable filter per session:
@Transactional
public List<Order> getActiveOrders() {
    Session session = em.unwrap(Session.class);
    session.enableFilter("softDeleteFilter").setParameter("deleted", false);
    return orderRepo.findAll();  // Automatically adds WHERE deleted=false
}
```

## Production Reality

For audit trails: use Spring Data `@EnableJpaAuditing` — it's the simplest and most integrated approach. For complex cross-entity concerns like soft deletes or multi-tenancy: Hibernate Filters are the right tool. For general interceptors: evaluate whether you actually need Hibernate-level interception versus a simpler Spring AOP aspect on your service layer.
